WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:20.032
ET: The mitotic spindle, the whole thing, the proteins and the chromosomes,

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:24.699
was isolated in 1952 by Mazia and Dan.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.999
And this brought forward the hope that we would be able

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.199
to understand what this was made of and maybe how it works.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.399
And there was a period of perhaps ten years in which many of us came,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.032
isolated spindles, tried to isolate from them the proteins,

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:45.632
and we failed because the structure was too complex. There were too many proteins.

00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.566
And we just didn't have an assay.

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:53.066
We knew that there was a fibrous protein, that is to say a filament,

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:58.166
because from the work of Shinye Inoue, he had shown that the spindle is birefringent.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:04.666
And therefore it must be, to some extent, a parallel array of filamentous proteins.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:07.332
And we wanted to find out what that filament was.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:11.499
And Shinye Inoue will also give a talk on this subject,

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.299
which I suggest strongly that you listen to as well.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:23.799
So we tried and failed both on sea urchin eggs and growing cells in culture

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:30.299
and isolating the proteins and nothing came out of it. There were too many things.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.766
So we had the idea that maybe we could use a drug called colchicine.

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:40.099
A whole book had been written on colchicine, and it was known to be specific,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.232
apparently specific, in blocking cell division,

00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:47.332
although there were controversies as to what this really meant.

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:51.999
Maybe it didn't block cell division, maybe it stimulated cell division.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.099
But everything suggested that this was a compound

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.566
that we might be able to use if a long shot suggestion

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:05.966
might be correct that this specific molecule

00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:09.966
was binding in some way, or interfering in some way,

00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:13.399
with the action of the formation of the spindle fibers.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.632
And so we set out to synthesize tritium labeled colchicine

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:26.032
because this would allow us to see if it was binding to something in the cell.

00:02:26.033 --> 00:02:29.732
This could be done by a method which was non specific, but easily done,

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:32.499
and we purified the tritium labeled colchicine.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.099
And indeed showed that it was taken up by cells in tissue culture,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.866
as well as into sea urchins eggs, which we had some sea urchins in the lab too,

00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:44.532
and that it was concentrated by the cell,

00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:48.466
which told us that it was binding to some component that was there

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.432
in relatively large amounts.

00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:55.999
It couldn't be an enzyme. It had to be, possibly, a structural protein.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.932
So it seemed like this was worth trying.

00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:03.866
It seemed, on the other hand, that the chances of this project succeeding were very, very small

00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:09.632
because it was a long shot idea, and I didn't like at first to give it to a graduate student,

00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:13.299
to work on because he could have wasted two or three years and got nowhere.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.266
But anyway, Gary Borisy joined the lab around that time

00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:22.999
and well, what did you want to do, Gary, when you got to the lab?

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.999
GB: I mean before getting on the project I was an undergrad at the University of Chicago

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.899
and Frank Child was one of my professors

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.166
and he was teaching biology and showed me how cells divide,

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:41.599
actually we saw living cells divide and I asked him rather naively,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.966
like all undergraduates are, I suppose, well how does it work?

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:47.266
What's the mechanism?

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:50.966
And it was clear that there was no molecular understanding

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:57.066
of the mechanism. And this is before I learned about your lab.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:04:01.032
I thought this is a fantastic problem to be studying,

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:06.032
a problem that is worth spending one's research career on.

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:10.099
And then after graduating and looking at graduate schools

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.266
I learned about your work with colchicine,

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:19.899
and I thought, "Oh my gosh, this is the molecular entree to the problem of mitosis."

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.199
So that is why I wanted to join the lab.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.932
ET: So then what we had to do was to get an assay,

00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:28.966
and that was to get something that bound to the protein,

00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:30.566
we could spin it out, separate it on a column,

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:35.199
later put it onto DEAE filter paper

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.832
and then what we could do would be to make an extract from a cell,

00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:43.766
spin off the particles, look at the protein,

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:46.999
and say well how much colchicine is bound.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.532
And then we could do what biochemists do,

00:04:49.533 --> 00:04:52.399
we fractionated into different test tubes,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.399
and tested each tube to see how much colchicine binding activity,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.032
and then we could go, because that was whole issue.

00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.266
We were trying to isolate the railroad tracks,

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:06.066
and there was no assay for railroad tracks.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:09.366
It is not an enzyme, so the whole trick here

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:13.666
was to find an assay. And it was a long shot bet, but it turned out

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:21.732
as the work went on and Gary, and also other people, Mike Shelanski, Dick Weisenberg,

00:05:21.733 --> 00:05:23.866
developed the approaches

00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:30.466
and then once we had figured out that it wasn't just in mitosis, but it was present all over,

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:35.099
and particularly in brain, and that was just, as we said,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:41.366
we were testing to see if it was a distribution of binding related to mitosis.

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:45.432
And the brain was the control, and that changed our thinking

00:05:45.433 --> 00:05:47.866
because clearly this was a widespread protein,

00:05:47.867 --> 00:05:50.566
which was in mitosis, but was all over the place.

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:55.666
GB: Well now we know that it's a widespread protein,

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:59.032
but at that time when we got the brain result it was very puzzling.

00:05:59.033 --> 00:05:59.766
ET: It was.

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:07.799
GB: Very puzzling. So the idea was to assay different tissues varying in mitotic index,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.966
and the expectation was those tissues high in mitotic index

00:06:10.967 --> 00:06:13.132
would have high colchicine binding,

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:16.832
brain very low in the mitotic index should be very low,

00:06:16.833 --> 00:06:19.532
and then we got this astounding result that brain was very high.

00:06:19.533 --> 00:06:25.266
And now the students at that time would meet around Botany Pond and talk about results,

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:30.099
and I remember very distinctly all, you know, my fellow students

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.032
were saying it's clearly an artifact

00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:37.666
of this drug partitioning into the membranes of nerve cells

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:41.832
because they are so rich in membrane structure for all the neuronal processes.

00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:43.499
So it's an artifact.

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.399
So we didn't immediately conclude from the brain result

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.232
that this was telling us something about the

00:06:49.233 --> 00:06:54.166
widespread nature of the colchicine binding protein, tubulin.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:55.799
We had to do further tests.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:02.499
One further test was to take advantage of a previous laboratory association

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:07.566
that you had, as you recall, when you had your adventure in Chile

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:11.999
and getting squid axoplasm from the marine station in Chile,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.532
and so you arranged to get some of that shipped up to us, and we tested it.

00:07:16.533 --> 00:07:20.666
And we thought that if there was something important in brain,

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:25.199
it might be even more abundant in the pure axoplasm,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.299
and in fact it was.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:32.432
And this would also provide a test for whether or not the drug was partitioning into the membranes

00:07:32.433 --> 00:07:35.632
because the membrane wouldn't be present in the axoplasm.

00:07:35.633 --> 00:07:41.299
And here this positive result gave us reassurance that

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:46.532
maybe it wasn't an artifact, this binding in brain, but it had a deeper significance.

00:07:46.533 --> 00:07:49.332
And it was around that time as I recall,

00:07:49.333 --> 00:07:53.399
that I came out to Woods Hole, or around that time.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.166
Maybe we don't have the sequence right.

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:59.932
But I came out to Woods Hole to learn how to isolate spindles

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:01.932
and extract protein from spindles,

00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:07.099
and we also learned how to isolate sperm tails and cilia and flagella

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.766
because those systems also have microtubules,

00:08:10.767 --> 00:08:13.832
and the word microtubules had only recently been coined

00:08:13.833 --> 00:08:17.066
because of the introduction of a new fixative that preserved them.

00:08:17.067 --> 00:08:18.599
Previously they hadn't been preserved.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:24.699
So I learned how to isolate mitotic spindles here

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.299
and extract the protein in a more gentle way

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.666
than had been done previously

00:08:28.667 --> 00:08:33.099
and found that we could identify the colchicine binding protein

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:34.832
with the same molecular properties.

00:08:34.833 --> 00:08:41.499
We used sedimentation at that time to identify how rapidly it moved in a centrifugal field,

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:47.366
so it had the same properties as the colchicine binding protein from cells and from brain.

00:08:47.367 --> 00:08:51.732
And so then we felt that there was something real here.

00:08:51.733 --> 00:08:53.532
We had this same molecule from these different sources,

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:59.432
and we asked what's the structural basis? Is there a structural basis for this?

00:08:59.433 --> 00:09:04.966
And the structure that we saw that was in common among all of these sources was the microtubule.

00:09:04.967 --> 00:09:08.666
And that's when we made that suggestion.

00:09:08.667 --> 00:09:11.966
ET: Yeah, because we hadn't been able to do the crucial experiment.

00:09:11.967 --> 00:09:15.066
The crucial experiment was to take this purified protein

00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:18.066
and turn it into microtubules. And we struggled.

00:09:18.067 --> 00:09:20.099
GB: And we struggled with that.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.532
ET: And I think we sometimes got some polymerization,

00:09:22.533 --> 00:09:24.899
but we never really were able to quantitate it.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:30.132
I am not sure, but I think you made-we had some microscopy,electron microscopy.

00:09:30.133 --> 00:09:32.066
We made rings, I think.

00:09:32.067 --> 00:09:40.099
GB: We made rings. There was also this confounding protein that we extracted from the spindles.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.166
This very large protein that had a cylindrical shape

00:09:44.167 --> 00:09:48.266
that at one time we thought could be the subunit of microtubules,

00:09:48.267 --> 00:09:50.232
but it turned out to be yolk protein.

00:09:50.233 --> 00:09:52.966
ET: Yeah, oh yes.

00:09:52.967 --> 00:09:55.366
GB: That was another red herring and blind alley in this study.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:10:01.099
Do you recall the discussion of the naming of this protein?

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.399
ET: Yes, we got scooped on that. We should have named the protein.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.132
GB: We should have named the protein, but do you recall that,

00:10:06.133 --> 00:10:10.632
I think it was in your office, and we were talking about what we should name the protein.

00:10:10.633 --> 00:10:15.866
I recall a discussion where we said, "Well, it's a microtubule

00:10:15.867 --> 00:10:23.032
so the subunit could be called tubulin."  With -in being the common suffix for a protein,

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:26.132
but it didn't sound very good to our ears,

00:10:26.133 --> 00:10:31.132
and we reverted to the name colchicine binding protein.

00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:37.632
Morhi, Hideo Morhi later felt that clearly the protein needed a name

00:10:37.633 --> 00:10:40.066
and so gave the protein the obvious name.

00:10:40.067 --> 00:10:43.832
But for several years we in the literature

00:10:43.833 --> 00:10:46.966
referred to it only as the colchicine binding protein.

00:10:46.967 --> 00:10:54.399
What do we take as messages that we might give to the students watching this?

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.166
ET: Well, you gave and I gave the same message: choose an important problem,

00:10:58.167 --> 00:11:02.666
and when you are just starting out choose what you think is the most important problem.

00:11:02.667 --> 00:11:06.832
I thought that this was the most important, and so did Gary when he joined the lab,

00:11:06.833 --> 00:11:09.932
and that is the first important thing.

00:11:09.933 --> 00:11:11.632
Don't work on a trivial problem.

00:11:11.633 --> 00:11:16.366
Do something where if you succeed, you have done something really good.

00:11:16.367 --> 00:11:22.566
And don't be confined in your thinking because we thought we were doing one thing,

00:11:22.567 --> 00:11:29.466
isolating something specific to mitosis, because of our thinking, which was, well colchicine

00:11:29.467 --> 00:11:33.199
is specifically blocking mitosis and therefore it is probably

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.499
some protein that's specific. And what we found as we went on,

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:41.532
we were completely wrong. It turned out to be not only general,

00:11:41.533 --> 00:11:45.632
but one of the most important fibrous proteins in the cell.

00:11:45.633 --> 00:11:53.099
GB: So I would add to that the observation that sometimes when you are doing work

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:57.499
you encounter apparently contradictory results.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.366
You encounter a paradox. Don't sweep it under the carpet.

00:12:00.367 --> 00:12:02.966
Look at it more deeply because

00:12:02.967 --> 00:12:07.766
sometimes the deepest understanding comes from resolving those paradoxes.

00:12:07.767 --> 00:12:16.366
Here we have Borisy/Taylor, Take 1.

00:12:16.367 --> 00:12:18.966
ET: Drop everything we are going to work on the brain.

00:12:18.967 --> 00:12:26.766
That's how I remember it, and then you, and well, Mike...

00:12:26.767 --> 00:12:28.532
GB: I think with, ok, see, so this is interesting.

00:12:28.533 --> 00:12:33.599
We may need to compare some notes and check some things for accuracy.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:40.966
My recollection is that the first hints of binding in brain

00:12:40.967 --> 00:12:44.232
actually came before I came to Woods Hole.

00:12:44.233 --> 00:12:51.599
And part of the motivation for coming to Woods Hole was to look at isolation of the spindle again to

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.132
try to resolve this paradoxical result with the brain tissue.

00:12:55.133 --> 00:13:00.266
GB: And then... ET: OK. GB: So we can check that, of course,

00:13:00.267 --> 00:13:05.199
if we just look at our old papers and notes.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.566
ET: We no longer have the notebooks.

00:13:07.567 --> 00:13:14.499
GB: So I thought that actually came first, but maybe it doesn't matter.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.466
Borisy/Taylor, Take 3.

00:13:16.467 --> 00:13:20.666
GB: I recall having to beg you to get onto the project

00:13:20.667 --> 00:13:27.132
because you said that it was not fit for a graduate student. It was too risky.

00:13:27.133 --> 00:13:28.566
ET: Yeah, well that is worth bringing up.

00:13:28.567 --> 00:13:29.599
GB: Do you remember that?

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.832
ET: I may have said that, yes...

00:13:32.833 --> 00:13:35.299
GB: Also, I had to beg to go to Woods Hole,

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:40.666
which I think you discouraged strongly because it would be, you know...

00:13:40.667 --> 00:13:42.232
ET: Yeah, letting your graduate student go away is..

00:13:42.233 --> 00:13:47.499
GB: You don't want to let the graduate student leave the lab. It is sort of a fundamental principle.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.532
Borisy/Taylor, Take 42.

00:13:49.533 --> 00:13:53.499
GB: You decided at some point, and I guess I never asked you this.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:58.466
You decided at some point that it was worth

00:13:58.467 --> 00:14:01.132
investigating the mechanism of action of that drug.

00:14:01.133 --> 00:14:05.966
What made you... what led you to that conclusion?

00:14:05.967 --> 00:14:10.732
ET: Yeah, well, gee, you should have taped this. This is better than what you are going to get.

00:14:10.733 --> 00:14:11.299
I have been.

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:12.866
GB: You have been?

00:14:12.867 --> 00:14:15.532
Absolutely. I tape everything.

00:14:15.533 --> 01:00:00.000
ET: Right well....

